{"reference":"John 18","book":"John","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Pilate and Roman Authority","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The exchange between the accusers and Pilate is not simply a private religious dispute. John places the authority to impose the death penalty within Roman prefectural power, while portraying negotiation between local leaders and the governor. An inscription independently preserves Pilate’s name and title as prefect of Judea, though it does not verify the particular hearing dialogue or identify the praetorium’s location.","verse_refs":["John 18:28-40"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_018_7c913d0627fce7c793fb6117"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"“The Jews” and Kingship","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"In this scene, the expression often rendered “the Jews” should not automatically be read as referring to all Jewish people. Its referent can vary with context, including particular authorities, Judeans, festival crowds, or Jewish people more broadly. That care matters here because Pilate’s repeated use of “King of the Jews” places Jesus’ kingship within a politically charged exchange shaped by imperial power.","verse_refs":["John 18:28-40"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_018_b6dbf02047ed69713bfced79"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Wider Passion Narrative","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Across the passion narrative, John depicts conflict involving particular Jewish leaders and participants, while Pilate orders a Roman crucifixion carried out by Roman soldiers. The narrative therefore cannot support claims of collective Jewish guilt or deicide.","verse_refs":["John 19:1-42"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_018_be99dd13424821e7946055f2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}